WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.040
YURI GRINSHTEYN: We try to
automate as much as possible

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.840
in our environments,
but we often treat

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.475
monitoring as an afterthought.

00:00:05.475 --> 00:00:08.100
Today, I'm going to show you how
to get started with automating

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.850
the setup of your
monitoring configuration

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:11.800
for a new project
using Terraform.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.140
This is "The Stack Doctor."

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.465
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:22.140
Let's start by
reviewing some basics.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.665
We need to cover
two major things--

00:00:23.665 --> 00:00:25.790
what should we automate
when setting up monitoring,

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.280
and how we can do that.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:31.820
Using the Cloud Monitoring API,
we can automate three things--

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:34.010
uptime checks, which are
automated probers that

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:36.673
can let us know if our
service is down or slow,

00:00:36.673 --> 00:00:38.840
alerting policies that
create incidents and generate

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.150
notifications, and
dashboards that

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:44.527
show us the state and
health of our systems.

00:00:44.527 --> 00:00:46.110
Now that we know
what we can automate,

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.850
the next question is how.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.930
Here, we also have
three options.

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:52.800
We can use the API directly,
we can use Cloud Deployment

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.930
Manager, or we can use
an open-source tool

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.100
like Terraform.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.340
Let's take a quick
look at each of those.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.260
Our first option is to use
the API or the gcloud command

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:03.412
to create our entities.

00:01:03.412 --> 00:01:04.870
For example, you
can make a call to

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:07.120
the uptimeCheckConfigs.create
method

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.922
to create a new uptime check.

00:01:08.922 --> 00:01:10.630
You can use the API
Explorer to determine

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:12.210
exactly how to make the call.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.830
Our recommendation would be to
use the API directly only when

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.260
no other options are
available, since tools

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:18.880
like Deployment
Manager and Terraform

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.980
make this much easier.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.680
Our second option is to use
Cloud Deployment Manager.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.750
It allows you to specify
all the resources needed

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:29.270
for your application in a
declarative format using

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.220
YAML files and then
execute them using

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:33.130
the gcloud command-line tool.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.630
You can also use templates to
parameterize the configuration

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:36.913
for better reuse.

00:01:36.913 --> 00:01:39.080
Deployment Manager has full
support for Google Cloud

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.173
services, and the
state or information

00:01:41.173 --> 00:01:42.590
about what's already
been deployed

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:44.610
is stored in the cloud.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.448
Our final option is Terraform.

00:01:46.448 --> 00:01:48.240
It's an open-source
product that allows you

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.520
to provision resources
across multiple clouds

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.410
through the use of providers.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:55.620
You define the resources you
want to provision in .TF files,

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:58.140
then use the Terraform
command-line tool to validate,

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.190
plan, and apply your deployment.

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:02.460
The state is stored locally.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.650
Now that we've outlined
both what we can automate

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.600
and what our options
for automation are,

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.467
let's take a look at
a specific example.

00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:11.009
Today, I want to show you how
to create an uptime check using

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:12.807
Terraform.

00:02:12.807 --> 00:02:14.890
You'll need to start by
downloading and installing

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:16.480
Terraform to your workstation.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.270
Note that, if you're using Cloud
Shell, it's installed for you.

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:21.880
You can use terraform
version to confirm.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.130
Next, let's create
our Terraform file.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.090
First, we'll specify
the provider.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.420
We want to use Google, and
we'll provide a default zone

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.820
and region for
compute resources.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:33.370
Next, we're going to define
the resource we're creating.

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:36.790
For our example, we're
creating an HTTPS uptime check

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:38.560
against the root
of the web server

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.920
on the standard HTTPS
port, which is 443.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.560
Next, we specify the
monitored resource.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.400
This is where we tell the uptime
check the host we're targeting.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.410
Note that we do not provide
the HTTP or HTTPS here.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:52.140
We've already specified that.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.087
Finally and optionally, we
can provide a text string.

00:02:55.087 --> 00:02:57.420
The uptime check will validate
that the content returned

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.460
from the server
includes that string

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.350
so that we can check
availability, latency,

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:04.180
and correctness all at once.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.460
We're now ready to
test and run our code.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.140
We're going to use
the Terraform init,

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:11.650
plan, and apply commands to
make sure our code is correct,

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.180
see exactly what
it's going to do,

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:15.660
and actually create the check.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:18.300
First, run terraform init
to install the Google Cloud

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:21.170
provider and initialize your
Terraform configuration.

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:22.920
Make sure you get the
confirmation message

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.660
that this step was successful.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.510
Next, run terraform plan
to confirm that the code is

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:28.830
going to do what you expect.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.200
In our case, it should
create a single new resource,

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.360
the uptime check we've defined.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.233
Finally, run terraform apply
to actually create the check.

00:03:36.233 --> 00:03:38.400
Note that you'll need to
explicitly confirm that you

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:39.900
want to perform this operation.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.820
At the end, confirm
that our operation

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.650
created the single new
resource by looking

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.700
at the output of the command.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:47.700
Next, head to the console
to see your uptime

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:48.710
check in the console.

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:50.460
Note that it may take
a few minutes for it

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.970
to start running
and returning data.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:54.515
Let's review what
we covered today.

00:03:54.515 --> 00:03:56.140
We learned that cloud
monitoring allows

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:58.690
us to automate the creation
of some common resources using

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:01.140
the monitoring configuration,
like uptime checks,

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.520
alerting policies,
and dashboards.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.260
We also reviewed
automation options

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:08.080
and looked at the monitoring
API, Cloud Deployment Manager,

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:09.250
and Terraform.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:11.320
Finally, we walked
through a specific example

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.515
of creating an uptime
check using Terraform.

00:04:13.515 --> 00:04:15.640
I hope you've found this
introduction to setting up

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.700
automation and treating your
monitoring as code useful.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:19.839
That's it for this episode.

00:04:19.839 --> 00:04:21.839
Come back next time when
we dive into automating

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:22.940
creating dashboards.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:24.130
This is The Stack Doctor.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:25.120
Stay healthy out there.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

